.SH TYPES
.SS Tokenizer
.IP
.EX
\f[B]pub\f[R] \f[B]enum\f[R] Tokenizer { Structure, Legacy }
.EE
.PP
Selects the tokenizer variant.
\f[B]Structure\f[R] (default) is the modern tokenizer that understands
\f[B]key=value\f[R] pairs, quoted strings, and JSON contexts.
\f[B]Legacy\f[R] is the original whitespace\-oriented tokenizer.
.PP
Call \f[B]Tokenizer::tokenize(line)\f[R] to produce a
\f[B]Vec<Token>\f[R], or use the free function \f[B]tokenize(line)\f[R]
as shorthand for \f[B]Tokenizer::Structure\f[R].
.SS Token / TokenType
.IP
.EX
\f[B]pub\f[R] \f[B]struct\f[R] Token { \f[B]pub\f[R] ty: TokenType, \f[B]pub\f[R] text: String }

\f[B]pub\f[R] \f[B]enum\f[R] TokenType {
    Literal   = 0,  \f[I]// static word/identifier\f[R]
    Delimiter = 1,  \f[I]// whitespace or punctuation\f[R]
    Timestamp = 2,  \f[I]// recognized date/time\f[R]
    Ipv4      = 3,  \f[I]// dotted\-quad IPv4\f[R]
    Quoted    = 4,  \f[I]// quoted string contents\f[R]
    Number    = 5,  \f[I]// numeric value\f[R]
    Json      = 6,  \f[I]// nested JSON value\f[R]
    JsonKey   = 7,  \f[I]// key in key=value or {\(dqkey\(dq:...}\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]TokenType::is_structural()\f[R] returns \f[B]true\f[R] for Literal,
JsonKey, and Json (tokens that contribute to the structure signature).
.PP
\f[B]TokenType::is_parameter()\f[R] returns \f[B]true\f[R] for Number,
Timestamp, Ipv4, and Quoted (dynamic tokens tracked per profile).
.SS structure_signature
.IP
.EX
\f[B]pub\f[R] \f[B]fn\f[R] structure_signature(tokens: &[Token]) \-> StructureHash
.EE
.PP
Computes the FNV\-1a 64\-bit hash of the structural tokens (type + text
for each, in order, with a separator byte).
Two lines with the same structure but different dynamic values produce
the same \f[B]StructureHash\f[R].
